CM_ SG_ 1797 Unknown_BMS_Sync_00_05 "00 or 05";
CM_ BO_ 1216 " Reserved (TIME, Serial Number from Charger). Datum/Zeit. Zeit und Seriennummer von Eigenbau-Ladegeraet";
CM_ BO_ 736 "Tuersteuergeraet";
CM_ BO_ 960 "Reserved (Infos from Charger). Infos von Eigenbau-Ladegeraet";
CM_ SG_ 704 DCDC_Warnings "warning:
5s after start = 00 -->01
values found: 0; 1; 4";
CM_ BO_ 576 "Befehl an Ladegeraet";
CM_ BO_ 448 "charger status recorded? nur beim Laden relevant. Ladegeraet Status";
CM_ SG_ 448 Charger_heatsink_temperature "Waermetauscher-Temperatur";
CM_ SG_ 448 Charger_temperature "Elektronik-Temperatur von charger ";
CM_ BO_ 658 "ECU status, error reports";
CM_ SG_ 658 Drive_mode "0 = ECO
2 = Sport
3 = Snow";
CM_ SG_ 657 trip_km "Factor 1 -> 0.1 (2026-08-05, user-confirmed): raw value is stored/sent in 100m steps, so divide by 10 to get km.";
CM_ SG_ 401 requested_torque "Factor 1 -> 0.005 Nm/count (2026-08-05, inferred, not firmware-confirmed -- can_io.c is the dashboard node and never builds/reads 0x191, see BO_401 comment below). Raw value clips at exactly +-10000 in every one of the 5 available driving traces regardless of route/driver, a classic torque-limiter clamp signature; the user confirmed the real vehicle's torque tops out at ~50Nm, so +-10000 counts = +-50Nm gives a clean 0.005 Nm/count. Shown alongside the raw count on its plot (right-hand axis) since this has no independent second measurement to cross-check against, unlike e.g. VoltageHV/skai_DC_Link_Voltage.";
CM_ SG_ 656 skai_torque "Factor 1 -> 0.005 Nm/count (2026-08-05, inferred, not firmware-confirmed): same field layout/message pairing as requested_torque (this is the SKAI's reported actual torque vs. the ECU's commanded one), and raw values reach the same +-10000 clip in the traces where the command saturates -- applied the identical 0.005 Nm/count for consistency between commanded and actual. can_io.c (case 0x290) reads this raw int16 but applies no scale of its own.";
CM_ SG_ 0 Heartbeat_00_05 "00/05";
CM_ BO_ 517 "Cycle Time 100ms";
CM_ SG_ 133 EMCY_ID "See CANopen EMCY service

//not shure if it gets sent by ECU or BMS";
CM_ BO_ 128 "Cycle Time 100ms";
CM_ BO_ 646 "cell_temperature recorded? nicht relevant fuer Fahrdaten";
CM_ BO_ 390 "battery_current, battery_voltage, SOC recorded?";
CM_ BO_ 774 "internal resistance recorded? nicht relevant fuer Fahrdaten";
CM_ SG_ 774 State_of_health "SoH ";
CM_ BO_ 518 "cell_voltage recorded? nicht relevant fuer Fahrdaten";
CM_ "Sign/scale corrections applied 2026-08-03 against can_io.c (SAM STM32 dashboard firmware, real RX decode) -- the original values below did not match how the firmware actually reads these bytes:
BO_390 Battery_voltage, Battery_current: unsigned->signed (int16_t mainVoltage/mainCurrentSlow, case 0x186)
BO_902 Battery_current: unsigned->signed (int16_t mainCurrentFast, case 0x386); dynamic_max_charge_current, dynamic_max_discharge_Current: signed->unsigned (uint16_t, case 0x386)
BO_1168 Wicklungstemperatur, Temperatur_T1, Temperatur_T3: unsigned->signed (int16_t, case 0x490)
BO_912 Motor_speed: unsigned->signed (int16_t skai.speed, case 0x390) -- this was the source of the bogus ~65535 rpm spikes
BO_448 Charger_temperature, Charger_heatsink_temperature: unsigned->signed (int8_t, case 0x1c0); VoltageLV: factor 1->0.00625 V/count (confirmed via firmware threshold check ((float)voltage12V*0.00625)>13.2)
BO_704 DCDC_HV_Current, DCDC_12V_Current: signed->unsigned (uint16_t current12V/currentMain, case 0x2c0)
BO_960 Ustopcharge, Outside_temp, Inside_temp: unsigned->signed (int16_t, case 0x3c0, uStopCharge sub-message). ID 0x3c0/960 is multiplexed in firmware between 3 different sub-messages selected by flag bits in byte1 (RxData[1]&0xC0); decodeMessages.m now filters to keep only frames where byte1's top bit is set (the sub-message this DBC layout matches) and, per firmware (RxData[1] &= ~0xc0;), masks off byte1's top 2 bits before decoding Ustopcharge, since those bits are the mux flags, not part of the value. Inside_temp/Outside_temp: factor 1->0.1 -- at factor 1 these read >200-370 C (impossible); can_io.c stores them raw with no visible scale applied elsewhere in that file, but 0.1 C/count produces 28-38 C, physically plausible for August ambient/enclosure temps. This factor is INFERRED, not read directly from can_io.c -- flag if it looks off. Ustopcharge's scale remains unconfirmed (factor 1, i.e. raw counts) -- no matching constant was found in can_io.c.
BO_657 Voltage_12V: signed->unsigned (plain uint8_t v12BusVoltage, case 0x291)
BO_390 SoC, remaining_capacity: firmware comments mark these fields as NOT used on the real vehicle -- it sources SoC from BO_1158 (case 0x486) and remaining energy from BO_774 (case 0x306) instead. Left decodable here for reference but the app now reads the BO_1158/BO_774 versions.
No firmware evidence was found for BO_401 ECU_Skai_Control (requested_torque/requested_speed/Skai_enable/Torque_or_Speed_Control) or BO_448 VoltageHV -- can_io.c is the dashboard/logger node and does not transmit or decode those. Left unchanged.
BO_657 acceleration_Pedal, break_pedal: signed->unsigned, added 2026-08-04. can_io.c's case 0x291 only reads bytes 4-5 (ignitionKey/v12BusVoltage) and never touches bytes 0-3, so this fix is INFERRED FROM DATA, not firmware-confirmed. Evidence: across all 5 driving traces in 'Normale Fahrten', the raw uint16 for acceleration_Pedal ramps smoothly and continuously through the full 0-65535 range every time the pedal is pressed noticeably (28-45% of frames land >=32768 in every single trace) -- decoded as signed this produces a discontinuous jump from ~+32767 to ~-32768 every time the pedal crosses that raw count, i.e. exactly the 'square wave' glitch seen in the Pedals plot. break_pedal shares the identical 16-bit field layout in the same message and was fixed for consistency, even though no trace so far presses the brake pedal hard enough (raw stayed under ~25000 in all 5 traces) to actually exercise the wraparound.
Scale factors added 2026-08-04, cross-validated against BMS_Battery_voltage_current_SoC.Battery_voltage across all 5 driving traces (can_io.c passes these fields through raw with no scale applied at case 0x290/0x1c0/0x2c0, so none of these are firmware-confirmed):
BO_656 skai_DC_Link_Voltage: factor 1->0.1. Ratio (BMS pack voltage / raw DC-link count) measured 0.0997 +/- 0.002 over 36k samples across all 5 trips -- i.e. essentially exactly 1/10 (matches the DC link directly reading the same HV pack the BMS reports, as expected for a motor inverter's DC bus). Reported as 1280 (should read 128.0 V) before this fix.
BO_448 VoltageHV: factor 1->0.0625. Ratio (BMS pack voltage / raw HV count) measured 0.06211 +/- 0.00001 (!) over the same 36k samples -- essentially exactly 1/16, i.e. the SAME per-count scale already used by BMS_Battery_voltage_current_SoC.Battery_voltage (0.0625 V/count) and exactly 10x the firmware-confirmed VoltageLV factor (0.00625). The ~0.6% gap between the raw empirical ratio and a clean 1/16 is well within the tolerance expected from two independent voltage sensors (charger ADC vs BMS shunt) plus resample timing.
BO_704 DCDC_12V_Current: factor 1->0.0625 A/count, by analogy with the 0.0625-per-count family above. Cross-checked against the ALREADY firmware-confirmed VoltageLV (0.00625 V/count, reads ~13.8V): 13.8V * (raw_current*0.0625) gives DCDC output power with median ~160W and range ~95-215W across trips during normal driving, matching the vehicle's known typical DCDC load. DCDC_HV_Current: same 0.0625 A/count factor applied by symmetry (same message, same 16-bit field format) but genuinely UNCONFIRMED -- it reads exactly 0 in all 5 available driving traces (0 nonzero frames out of ~36k), so there is no data to validate any scale against; it is possibly not the DCDC's own HV-side draw at all but the AC mains charging current, which would only be nonzero during a charge session (no v2.0-format charging trace is available to check -- see 'Known limitations' in PROJECT_NOTES.md).
BO_658 FECU_error, fixed 2026-08-04, FIRMWARE-CONFIRMED (can_io.c case 0x292: ecu.errorMsg = (*(uint16_t*)&RxData[6]) | (RxData[5]<<16), which on this little-endian target expands to RxData[6] | (RxData[7]<<8) | (RxData[5]<<16) -- byte5 is the MSB, byte6 the LSB, byte7 the middle byte). The DBC's old plain-intel 24-bit SG_ (byte5|byte6<<8|byte7<<16) decoded a completely different, wrong value: verified against all 5 driving traces, every single frame decoded as raw 0x00020 under the old (buggy) byte order versus the firmware-correct 0x200000 (no PDC on CAN, per SAM2_FECU_Errorhandling.pdf) -- exactly matching a user report of seeing error 20 in the app when the real error was 200000. Because this byte permutation isn't representable as one contiguous Intel/Motorola SG_, FECU_error has no SG_ line anymore -- it's reconstructed directly from raw bytes in decodeMessages.m (see comment there).";
BA_DEF_ "BusType" STRING ;
BA_DEF_DEF_ "BusType" "CAN";
VAL_ 390 Battery_status 32 "Error" 15 "not connected" 7 "Caution bad zero offset" 6 "U-Charge" 5 "Charge" 2 "Discharge" ;
VAL_ 658 Drive_mode 0 "ECO mode" 2 "Sport" 3 "Snow" ;
